🎗️ 26.3 Identify Shared Resource

🔴 The shared resource is: private int number;
🔴 Both threads access and modify the same number.

Example:

number = 1
    ↓
Odd Thread prints 1
    ↓
number = 2
    ↓
Even Thread prints 2
    ↓
number = 3

Therefore, number must belong to one shared object.

🎗️ 26.4 Identify the Concurrency Problem

👉 Race Condition
🔴 Both threads modify: number++;
🔴 Without synchronization, both threads could access the variable at the same time.

Therefore:

Shared Mutable State
        ↓
Race Condition Possible
        ↓
Synchronization Required

👉 Mutual Exclusion
🔴 Only one thread should execute the critical section at a time.
🔴 We achieve this using: synchronized

🎗️ 26.5 Choose the Synchronization Mechanism

We use:

public synchronized void printEven()
public synchronized void printOdd()

🔴 Both methods belong to the same SharedResource object.
🔴 Therefore, both methods synchronize on the same monitor:

SharedResource Object
        ↓
      Monitor
      ↙     ↘
printEven()  printOdd()

🔴 Only one thread can execute the synchronized method on this object at a time.

🎗️ 26.6 Thread Conditions

👉 Even Thread
🔴 Even thread should print only when: number % 2 == 0
🔴 Therefore, if the number is odd: it must wait for it's turn

while(number % 2 != 0) {
    wait();
}


👉 Odd Thread
🔴 Odd thread should print only when: number % 2 != 0
🔴 Therefore, if the number is even: it must wait for it's turn

while(number % 2 == 0) {
    wait();
}

👉 Summary

Even Thread
    ↓
Number is Odd?
    ↓
YES → wait()
    ↓
Number is Even
    ↓
Print


Odd Thread
    ↓
Number is Even?
    ↓
YES → wait()
    ↓
Number is Odd
    ↓
Print

🎗️ 26.7 Why wait()?

🔴 Suppose: number = 2 and the Odd Thread enters.
🔴 It is not the Odd Thread's turn. Instead of continuously checking:

while(number % 2 == 0) {

}

🔴 which causes busy waiting, we use: wait();

👉 wait():

🔴 Puts the thread into waiting state.
🔴 Releases the object's monitor.
🔴 Allows the other thread to acquire the monitor.

🎗️ 26.8 Why while Instead of if?

👉 Wrong:

if(number % 2 == 0) {
    wait();
}

👉 Correct:

while(number % 2 == 0) {
    wait();
}

👉 Reason
🔴 After waking up, the thread must re-check the condition.

👉 This protects against:
🔴 Spurious wakeups.
🔴 Another thread changing the shared state before the awakened thread reacquires the monitor.

👉 Therefore:

wait()
  ↓
Wake Up
  ↓
Re-check Condition
  ↓
Still not my turn?
  ↓
wait() again

🎗️ 26.9 Why notify()?

🔴 After printing, the current thread changes the shared state: number++;
🔴 Now it should wake the other waiting thread: notify();

👉 Example:

Odd Thread
    ↓
Print 1
    ↓
number = 2
    ↓
notify()
    ↓
Even Thread wakes
    ↓
Print 2

🔴 Because this problem has only two threads, notify() can be used to wake the waiting thread.

🎗️ 26.10 Why number++ Before notify()?

👉 Correct:

number++;
notify();

🔴 The shared state should be updated before waking the other thread.

👉 Example:

number = 1

Odd prints 1
     ↓
number = 2
     ↓
notify()
     ↓
Even Thread checks
     ↓
2 is even
     ↓
Print 2

🔴 The waiting thread wakes up and sees the updated state.

🎗️ 26.11 Algorithm

👉 Even Thread
🔴 Acquire SharedResource monitor.
🔴 Check whether number <= limit.
🔴 If number is odd, wait.
🔴 If number is even, print it.
🔴 Increment number.
🔴 Notify the other thread.
🔴 Repeat until number > limit.

👉 Odd Thread
🔴 Acquire SharedResource monitor.
🔴 Check whether number <= limit.
🔴 If number is even, wait.
🔴 If number is odd, print it.
🔴 Increment number.
🔴 Notify the other thread.
🔴 Repeat until number > limit.

🎗️ 26.12 Implementation

👉 AlterenateEvenOddPrint.java

public class AlterenateEvenOddPrint {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SharedResource sharedResource = new SharedResource(1, 10);

        Thread thread1 = new EvenThread(sharedResource);
        Thread thread2 = new OddThread(sharedResource);

        thread1.setName("Even-Thread");
        thread2.setName("Odd-Thread");

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}

👉 OddThread.java

public class OddThread extends Thread {
    private final SharedResource sharedResource;

    public OddThread(SharedResource sharedResource) {
        this.sharedResource = sharedResource;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        sharedResource.printOdd();
    }
}

👉 EvenThread.java

public class EvenThread extends Thread {
    private final SharedResource sharedResource;

    public EvenThread(SharedResource sharedResource) {
        this.sharedResource = sharedResource;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        sharedResource.printEven();
    }
